     Question

   The Nautilus DevOps team is working to deploy some tools in Kubernetes cluster. Some of the tools are licence based so that licence information needs to be stored securely within Kubernetes cluster. Therefore, the team wants to utilize Kubernetes secrets to store those secrets. Below you can find more details about the requirements:
We already have a secret key file ecommerce.txt under the /opt/ directory. 
 1.Create a generic secret named ecommerce, it should contain the password/license-number present in ecommerce.txt file.
 2.Also create a pod named secret-datacenter.
 3.Configure pod's spec as container name should be secret-container-datacenter, image should be ubuntu with latest tag (remember to mention the tag with image). Use sleep command for container so that it remains in running state. Consume the created secret and mount it under /opt/games within the container.
 4.To verify you can exec into the container secret-container-datacenter, to check the secret key under the mounted path /opt/games. Before hitting the Check button please make sure pod/pods are in running state, also validation can take some time to complete so keep patience.

                Solution
 1. To create the pod
     kubectl create secret generic ecommerce \ --from-file=/opt/ecommerce.txt
Explanation
- kubectl create secret generic ecommerce → creates a secret named ecommerce.
- --from-file=/opt/ecommerce.txt → loads the contents of ecommerce.txt into the secret.
By default, the key will be the filename (ecommerce.txt) and the value will be the file’s content (your license/password)
 2-Create the pod
    vi secret-pod.yaml
 3-Configue the pod
 apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: secret-datacenter
spec:
  containers:
    - name: secret-container-datacenter
      image: ubuntu:latest
      command: [ "sleep", "3600" ]   # Keeps the container running
      volumeMounts:
        - name: ecommerce-secret
          mountPath: /opt/games
          readOnly: true
  volumes:
    - name: ecommerce-secret
      secret:
        secretName: ecommerce
  -Apply it:
    kubectl apply -f secret-pod.yaml
 4- Verification
   1. 	Check pod status
Make sure the pod is running:
     kubectl get pods
- You should see secret-datacenter with status Running.
   2.  Exec into the container
Run the following command to open a shell inside the container:
     kubectl exec -it secret-datacenter -- bash
- (If bash isn’t available in the Ubuntu image, you can use sh instead.)
   3.   Navigate to the mounted path
Inside the container:
    cd /opt/games
    ls -l
- You should see the secret file (e.g., ecommerce.txt or license-number depending on how you created the secret).
   4.   View the secret content
    cat /opt/games/ecommerce.txt
- or, if you used the custom key name:
    cat /opt/games/license-number





